<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/advanced/psychometrics-and-testing">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Psychometrics is <strong>the mathematics of measuring invisible things</strong> — traits,
    abilities, attitudes. Its instruments (reliability, validity, standardisation) are what
    separate measurement from opinion, and its history contains psychology's most instructive
    failures. Every survey, exam and clinical questionnaire runs on this discipline.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the measurement standards and their hardest case — IQ" widget="equationStep" config={psychCfg} />

  <h2>Measurement concepts with everyday leverage</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Regression to the mean:</strong> extreme scores drift toward average on retest — the reason "sophomore slumps" and "second-album flops" happen without any cause at all (the statistics ladder covers the maths).</li>
    <li><strong>Base rates and testing:</strong> a 95%-accurate test misleads when the condition is rare — screening debates (medical and security) live on this curve (the Bayesian lesson's example).</li>
    <li><strong>Item response theory:</strong> modern tests adapt to the respondent (GRE-style adaptive testing) — estimating ability from fewer, better-chosen items.</li>
    <li><strong>Self-report humility:</strong> people can't always report their own motives (Nisbett & Wilson, 1977). Behavioural measures and informant reports outperform self-knowledge for some traits — a permanent check on introspection.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Anastasi & Urbina, Psychological Testing; Flynn (1984, 2009); Nisbett & Wilson (1977).</p>
</TopicPage>
